// A sister reached by a path to her file, which is refused -- and the refusal
// closes a MISCOMPILE rather than adding a diagnostic.
//
// The file registry is keyed by the path, and the path is canonical now, so the
// spelling below reaches the entry the folder sweep made for beta instead of
// missing it. It used to miss it: a second module was built from beta's files,
// both spelled the same symbols, LLVM renamed the second, and the call here was
// left referencing a declaration nothing defined.
//
// Canonicalizing closes the duplicate and opens the sideways reach in one
// stroke, so the two are one decision. A neighbour is reached because the
// registry holds her name -- 'import beta' -- never because a path walked to her
// file: under the scoped rule the modules a path can reach are exactly the ones
// that must not resolve, and which of them it hits would otherwise be an accident
// of how the path was spelled.

mod alpha;

import "../beta/beta";     //~ ErrorModReach "reached by its name"

fn reach(n i64) i64 {
  n;
}
